<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deir el-Medina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1550–1080 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Also known as Dayr al-Madīnah, Set maat, Place of Truth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deir el-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> is an ancient Egyptian village on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, across from modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, that housed the artisans who built the tombs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a> during the 18th to 20th Dynasties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. Its ancient name was Set maat, Place of Truth, and at its peak it had around 68 houses. Excavated from 1922 to 1951 by a French team under Bernard Bruyère, it yielded thousands of ostraca and papyri recording letters, sales, prayers and work, making it one of the best-documented communities of the ancient world. Around 1170 BCE, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>, its workmen staged what may have been the first recorded strike. It was abandoned c. 1110–1080 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-xi">Ramesses XI</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deir-el-medina.jpg" alt="Deir el-Medina" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deir_el-Medina,_Luxor,_Egipto,_2022-04-03,_DD_18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
